## 12. Indicative implementation roadmap

### Closing to month 3: stabilize and establish control

- Confirm regulatory commitments in each jurisdiction;
- Meet supervisors and present the integration governance model;
- Protect critical employees and vendors;
- Complete cyber and operational-resilience diagnostics;
- Freeze unnecessary high-risk change;
- Inventory systems, products, data, contracts, and regulatory reports;
- Establish country and group integration offices;
- Define management decision rights;
- Launch daily service and liquidity monitoring;
- Retain local brands.

### Months 3–9: design and control harmonization

- Select the target operating and technology architecture;
- Remediate urgent control weaknesses;
- Establish group cybersecurity and financial-crime minimum standards;
- Define the common data model and API architecture;
- Complete customer and brand research;
- Assess leadership and confirm country teams;
- Submit required outsourcing, data, and technology plans;
- Build the migration factory and testing standards;
- Pilot common digital components internally.

### Months 6–18: digital convergence

- Release common digital services by country in controlled waves;
- Preserve local product and payment configurations;
- Introduce endorsed branding;
- Establish common customer-service metrics;
- Pilot shared services;
- Cleanse migration data;
- Complete target-core selection;
- Select the first migration country based on readiness;
- Conduct regulator-observed testing or demonstrations where useful.

### Months 12–24: first core migration

- Migrate a limited segment or product first where practical;
- Reconcile balances, transactions, interest, and regulatory reports;
- Complete a full-country cutover only after all gates pass;
- Maintain enhanced post-cutover monitoring;
- Commission independent assurance;
- postpone major rebranding until stability is demonstrated.

### Months 18–30: second-country migration and selective consolidation

- Apply lessons from the first migration;
- Rationalize selected products and vendors;
- Expand common digital and data services;
- Implement approved shared-service arrangements;
- Decide each country’s long-term brand architecture;
- Begin carefully sequenced branch or operating-model consolidation.

### Months 24–36 and beyond: third migration and optimization

- Complete the remaining core migration if the business case still holds;
- Remove decommissioned systems only after record-retention and audit requirements are satisfied;
- Complete selected brand transitions;
- Optimize workforce and vendor structures;
- Expand regional corporate and cross-border services;
- Review concentration risk created by the common platform;
- Maintain country-specific resilience and exit capability.

The roadmap should remain milestone-based. A country should move only when its regulator, data, systems, employees, and customers are ready.

