## 13. Measure integration success broadly

### Regulatory and risk metrics

- Acquisition commitments completed;
- Regulatory findings and overdue actions;
- Capital and liquidity headroom;
- Regulatory-reporting accuracy;
- Financial-crime backlogs;
- Cyber incidents and remediation time;
- Critical-service availability;
- Outsourcing and data-transfer compliance.

### Technology metrics

- Digital transaction success rates;
- Core and payment availability;
- API response and failure rates;
- Reconciliation exceptions;
- Data-quality scores;
- Release failure and rollback rates;
- Disaster-recovery performance;
- Number and cost of legacy systems retired.

### Customer metrics

- Deposit retention;
- Active digital customers;
- Failed transactions;
- Complaint volumes and resolution times;
- Fraud losses;
- Customer trust and brand awareness;
- Account closure and attrition rates;
- Service performance by language, region, and customer segment;
- Access for vulnerable and non-digital customers.

### People and culture metrics

- Retention of critical employees;
- Leadership succession coverage;
- Employee engagement;
- Speak-up and conduct indicators;
- Training completion;
- Local versus group talent representation;
- Decision and issue-resolution speed.

### Financial metrics

- Integration spending against approved business case;
- Realized versus projected synergies;
- Revenue retention;
- Cost-to-income ratio;
- Digital cost to serve;
- Vendor savings;
- Credit-loss and fraud trends;
- Return on invested integration capital.

Executive incentives should balance synergies with regulatory outcomes, service stability, customer retention, employee conduct, and successful control remediation.

