## Key decisions and recommended posture

| Decision | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| Legal entities | Preserve locally regulated subsidiaries unless a separate legal consolidation has a compelling case |
| Digital products | One reusable platform with country-specific configurations |
| Core systems | Select strategically; migrate one country at a time |
| Customer data | Federated by default; centralize only when lawful and necessary |
| Brand | Begin with endorsed local brands; decide final architecture by country |
| Management | Retain credible local leadership, clarify decision rights, and assess objectively |
| Risk standards | Apply a group minimum plus stricter local requirements |
| Shared services | Introduce selectively under enforceable service and resilience arrangements |
| Product consolidation | Rationalize only after customer-impact and regulatory review |
| Branch consolidation | Sequence after digital stability and access analysis |
| Regulatory engagement | Separate country plans with consistent group transparency |
| Migration timing | Gate-based rather than driven by public deadlines |

## Conclusion

The acquisition should be integrated as a **federated pan-African banking group with common technology and risk foundations but locally accountable regulated banks**.

The critical sequencing is:

1. Stabilize customers, controls, liquidity, and key staff;
2. Establish separate regulator-approved country plans;
3. Harmonize risk, cybersecurity, data, and governance;
4. Place a configurable common digital layer over the existing cores;
5. Migrate cores one country at a time using strict reconciliation and rollback gates;
6. Retain endorsed local brands until service stability and customer evidence justify further convergence;
7. Align management through explicit accountability, objective assessment, and protected local control functions;
8. Consolidate products, branches, vendors, and shared services only where customer access, resilience, and regulation permit.

This approach will realize digital and operating synergies more slowly than a big-bang consolidation, but it materially reduces the risks of payment disruption, deposit attrition, regulatory intervention, cultural resistance, and damage to the group’s emerging regional brand.
